Chapter 10
Covering the Fixed Charges
تغطية الأعباء الثابتة
The leverage ratios of Chapter 9 measure a stock against a stock: debt on the balance sheet against equity on the balance sheet. They say how much is owed and nothing about whether it can be serviced. A company with debt of $1,000,000 and earnings of $50,000 and a company with debt of $1,000,000 and earnings of $5,000,000 have identical leverage ratios and entirely different prospects.
Coverage ratios close that gap. They measure a flow against a flow: the earnings or the cash of a year against the charges that year has to meet. For a long-term lender they are the more important of the two families, and the Learning Outcome Statements name three.
Figure F510-01 is your map.
Figure F510-01. Chapter 10 roadmap: three coverage ratios, from earnings to cash.
