SC10-1  Times interest earned divides:
A.	net income by interest expense.
B.	earnings before interest and taxes by interest expense.
C.	operating income by total debt.
D.	operating cash flow by interest paid.
SC10-2  Why is tax added back in the numerator?
A.	Because tax is not an expense
B.	Because interest is deducted before tax, so the company need not earn the tax on it
C.	Because tax is paid in the following year
D.	Because the ratio is computed on a cash basis
SC10-3  Orontes's EBIT is 4,348,800 and interest is 390,000. Coverage is:
A.	11.15 times
B.	10.15 times
C.	0.09 times
D.	1.10 times
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
10.2  Fixed charge coverage
Interest is not the only charge a company must meet whatever happens. Rent under an operating lease must be paid. So must the lease payments on equipment, preferred dividends in some definitions, and in many agreements the principal instalments too.
Fixed charge coverage, which the Learning Outcome Statements also call earnings to fixed charges, widens the ratio to take those in. The standard form adds the interest element of lease rentals to both the numerator and the denominator, because that element was deducted in arriving at earnings and has to be added back before it is counted as a charge.
Orontes pays operating lease rentals of $240,000 a year on its Gulf warehouses. The conventional treatment takes one-third of a rental as its interest element, which here is 240,000 ÷ 3 = 80,000.
Fixed charges are therefore interest of $390,000 plus the lease element of $80,000: 390,000 + 80,000 = 470,000.
The numerator is EBIT plus the same lease element: 4,348,800 + 80,000 = 4,428,800.
Fixed charge coverage is 4,428,800 ÷ 470,000 = 9.42 times.
Figure F510-03 sets the two ratios side by side.
Figure F510-03. Orontes: interest coverage and fixed charge coverage
@table
| Numerator | Denominator | Ratio
Interest coverage | EBIT 4,348,800 | Interest 390,000 | 11.15
Fixed charge coverage | EBIT plus lease element 4,428,800 | Interest plus lease element 470,000 | 9.42
@end
Fixed charge coverage is always the lower of the two, because the denominator widens by more, proportionately, than the numerator. The gap between them is the measure of how much of a company's fixed burden is something other than interest. For Orontes the gap is small, because it owns most of its plant. For a retailer that leases every store, the gap is enormous, and a lender who looked only at interest coverage would badly misjudge it.
EXAM TRAP   Fixed charge coverage traps
Add the lease element to BOTH halves. Adding it only to the denominator understates the ratio.
A preferred dividend, where a question includes it, must be grossed up for tax: it is paid out of after-tax income, so the pre-tax earnings needed are the dividend divided by one minus the tax rate.
Principal repayments are included in some definitions and not in others. The exam says which.
LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Charge" in English
English charge has several accounting senses: an expense in the income statement, an obligation to pay, and a security given over an asset. A fixed charge in this chapter means an obligation that must be met whatever the level of activity. Arabic عبء ثابت and French charge fixe both carry the expense sense; the obligation sense has to be learned.
