Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
10.3  Cash flow to fixed charges
Earnings are an accounting measure and the fixed charges are paid in money. A company can report earnings that cover its interest comfortably and still not have the cash, because the earnings are in receivables and inventory. The third ratio removes that risk by replacing earnings with cash.
Cash flow to fixed charges divides operating cash flow, before the fixed charges and before tax, by the fixed charges. In practice the numerator is operating cash flow as reported, plus the fixed charges that were deducted in arriving at it, plus income taxes paid.
Orontes's operating cash flow for 2025 is $7,531,100. It paid interest of $390,000 and income taxes of $839,700, both of which are deducted in arriving at that figure under U.S. GAAP, and the lease element of $80,000 is inside the rental expense. Adding the fixed charges of $470,000 and the taxes paid of $839,700 back:
the numerator is 7,531,100 + 470,000 + 839,700, which is 8,840,800. Cash flow to fixed charges is 8,840,800 ÷ 470,000 = 18.81 times.
That is very nearly twice the earnings-based figure, and the reason is the one Chapter 5 established: Orontes's earnings this year carry a large non-cash charge. The impairment of $2,724,000 reduced EBIT and took no cash. On a cash basis the company covers its fixed charges nineteen times over.
Figure F510-04 sets all three out.
Figure F510-04. Orontes: the three coverage ratios
@table
Ratio | 2025 | What it adds to the one above
Interest coverage | 11.15 | —
Fixed charge coverage | 9.42 | The lease rentals that must be paid
Cash flow to fixed charges | 18.81 | Cash in place of earnings
@end
A lender reads them in that order and pays most attention to the third, because the third is what actually arrives in the bank. When the third is far below the first, earnings are not converting to cash and the lender is being told something the leverage ratios cannot say.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: when cash coverage is the lower figure
Step 1. Suppose a company reports EBIT of $5,000,000 and interest of $1,000,000, so interest coverage is 5.00 times.
Step 2. But receivables rose $3,000,000 and inventory rose $1,500,000 during the year, so operating cash flow is only $1,200,000.
Step 3. Cash coverage, adding back interest of $1,000,000 and taxes paid of $400,000, is 2,600,000 ÷ 1,000,000 = 2.60 times.
Check. Coverage of 5.00 on earnings and 2.60 on cash describes a company whose profit is sitting in working capital. Orontes is the opposite case: 11.15 on earnings and 18.81 on cash. The direction of the gap is the finding, not its size.
