10.4  Coverage and the lender's decision
A lender uses the three ratios together, and it uses them forwards rather than backwards.
The historical figure establishes the starting point. Orontes covers interest 11.15 times today.
The pro forma figure is what matters. The lender recomputes coverage on the facility it is being asked to grant, at the interest rate it would charge, assuming the project contributes nothing in the first year. That was the worked example of section 10.1: 3.92 times.
The stressed figure decides it. The lender then applies the operating leverage of Chapter 8. Orontes's DOL is 4.02, so a 10 per cent fall in sales cuts operating income 40.2 per cent. Take EBIT down by the same proportion, to about 2,600,000, and coverage on the enlarged interest becomes roughly 2,600,000 ÷ 1,110,000 = 2.34 times. That is the number the credit committee argues about.
YOUR TURN   Build the lender's three figures
Figure F510-05 gives the structure. Complete it. Answers are at the end of the chapter.
Figure F510-05. Your turn: coverage as a lender computes it (complete the blanks)
@table
Basis | EBIT | Interest | Coverage
As reported, 2025 | 4,348,800 | 390,000 | 11.15
After borrowing 12,000,000 at 6 per cent | 4,348,800 | ________ | ________
Stressed: sales down 10 per cent, DOL 4.02 | 2,600,000 | 1,110,000 | ________
@end
Note the asymmetry the exercise exposes. The leverage ratios of Chapter 9 would move from an equity multiplier of 1.55 to about 2.14 — a change a shareholder might welcome. The coverage ratio moves from 11.15 to 2.34 under stress, which is the same decision seen from the lender's side. Neither party is wrong; they are measuring different risks with different instruments.
EXAM TRAP   Coverage is forward-looking
A question that gives you a proposed borrowing wants the coverage AFTER it, not before.
If the question gives you a degree of operating leverage and a fall in sales, it wants the stressed figure.
Assume a project contributes nothing in its first year unless the question says otherwise.
