10.5  What coverage ratios miss
Three things, all examinable.
They measure one year. A ratio of 11.15 computed on a year containing a large one-off gain says less than the same ratio computed on three years. Where the exam gives a series, use it.
They assume the charge is the charge. Floating-rate debt changes the denominator without any decision by the company, and a covenant tested quarterly can be breached by an interest-rate movement alone.
They ignore when the principal falls due. A company covering interest fifteen times with the whole principal repayable in eighteen months is in more danger than one covering it four times over a twenty-year schedule. The ratio sees the interest and not the maturity, which is why Chapter 9 insisted on reading the borrowings note.
Figure F510-06 lists them.
Figure F510-06. Three things a coverage ratio does not see
@table
Limit | Why it matters | Where it is answered
One year of earnings | A one-off gain or charge distorts it | Use a series; Chapter 17 on earnings quality
The rate may move | Floating-rate debt changes the denominator | The borrowings note
The maturity is invisible | Interest is covered; the principal still falls due | The maturity profile, Chapter 9
@end
TERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section
@table
English (exam term) | العربية | Français
floating rate | سعر فائدة متغير | taux variable
maturity | تاريخ الاستحقاق | échéance
refinancing | إعادة التمويل | refinancement
@end
