@end
Multiple-choice questions
P10-01  Answer C. The ratio uses earnings before interest and taxes.
A is wrong. Net income is struck after interest.
B is wrong. Operating income excludes other income and expense.
D is wrong. Operating cash flow is used in the cash coverage ratio.
P10-02  Answer A. 4,348,800 ÷ 390,000 = 11.15.
B is wrong. 9.42 is fixed charge coverage.
C is wrong. 18.81 is cash coverage.
D is wrong. 1.10 is the degree of financial leverage.
P10-03  Answer B. Fixed charges are obligations that must be met whatever the level of activity.
A is wrong. Tax is not a fixed charge.
C is wrong. Depreciation is not paid.
D is wrong. Common dividends are discretionary.
P10-04  Answer C. One-third of 240,000 is 80,000.
A is wrong. The whole rental is not the interest element.
B is wrong. This is half, not a third.
D is wrong. This is a tenth.
P10-05  Answer B. 4,428,800 ÷ 470,000 = 9.42.
A is wrong. 11.15 is interest coverage.
C is wrong. 18.81 is cash coverage.
D is wrong. 3.92 is coverage after the new borrowing.
P10-06  Answer A. Non-cash charges reduce earnings and not cash, so the cash ratio is higher.
B is wrong. Growing receivables reduce cash coverage.
C is wrong. A lower rate raises both ratios.
D is wrong. A share issue is a financing item.
P10-07  Answer B. Earnings are not converting, and working capital is where they usually sit.
A is wrong. An impairment raises cash coverage relative to earnings coverage.
C is wrong. A share issue does not affect either numerator.
D is wrong. Reducing debt raises both.
P10-08  Answer B. Interest becomes 1,110,000 and 4,348,800 ÷ 1,110,000 = 3.92.
A is wrong. That is the figure before borrowing.
C is wrong. 2.34 is the stressed figure.
D is wrong. 9.42 is fixed charge coverage before borrowing.
P10-09  Answer C. Preferred dividends are paid out of after-tax income.
A is wrong. Interest is deductible and is not grossed up.
B is wrong. Rentals are deductible.
D is wrong. Depreciation is not a fixed charge for this purpose.
P10-10  Answer B. Coverage measures the charge for the year, not when the principal is repayable.
A is wrong. The interest charge is the denominator.
C is wrong. EBIT is the numerator.
D is wrong. Lease rentals are in fixed charge coverage.
Case-style set: two borrowers
Orontes. Interest coverage is 4,348,800 ÷ 390,000 = 11.15 times. Its lease element is 240,000 ÷ 3 = 80,000, so fixed charges are 390,000 + 80,000 = 470,000 and the numerator is 4,348,800 + 80,000 = 4,428,800, giving fixed charge coverage of 9.42 times.
Levant Stores. Interest coverage is 4,200,000 ÷ 300,000 = 14.00 times, which is better than Orontes. Its lease element is 3,600,000 ÷ 3 = 1,200,000, so fixed charges are 300,000 + 1,200,000 = 1,500,000 and the numerator is 4,200,000 + 1,200,000 = 5,400,000, giving fixed charge coverage of 5,400,000 ÷ 1,500,000 = 3.60 times.
So the retailer looks stronger on interest coverage and much weaker on fixed charge coverage, and the second figure is the honest one: its stores are leased, and the rent is as unavoidable as interest.
I would lend to Orontes, but the reason is not only the coverage. Orontes's degree of operating leverage is 4.02 against the retailer's 1.6, so a fall in sales hurts Orontes's earnings two and a half times as hard. Against that, Orontes starts from 9.42 times cover and the retailer from 3.60. A 10 per cent fall in sales takes Orontes to roughly 5.6 times and the retailer to roughly 3.0 times. Orontes is the safer borrower at every point in the cycle, and it is safer by a wider margin in a bad year than in a good one.
